-- title:
--   Round six: final reduction from a machine with the round-six cost to KappaBound
-- statement:
--   Let $P$ satisfy `Prm.Ok` and let $\kappa < \mathrm{gap}(P)$. Let $M$ be a multitape Turing machine that multiplies correctly on every input length $n \ge 1$, and suppose there are $n_1, A \in \mathbb N$ and $C_0 \ge 0$ such that for every $n \ge n_1$ the machine multiplies $n$-bit numbers within time
--
--   $$C_0\big(V(P,n)\, N(P,n)\, (\log \lg n + 1)^{A} + n\big),$$
--
--   where $V$ is the round-six bit volume and $N$ the per-bit cost. Then the platform bound `KappaBound` $\kappa$ holds, i.e. integer multiplication is possible in time $O(n \log n \cdot \text{(saving } \lg^{-\kappa}))$ in the sense of `Def_IntMul_MultitapeModel`.
--
--   This is the glue between the round-six machine (still to be built) and the campaign's exponent bookkeeping.
--
--   In Lean:
